<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Fast DDS profiles for the cockpit's ROS 2 stack. Three fixes live here: two for
  ways nav2 fails to come up with no error of its own, and one for a depth
  camera's frames, which lyrical delivered a third of. Every process
  that runs ROS in the robot image gets this file through
  FASTDDS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE (docker-compose.yml, docker/Dockerfile, and
  runners.py / one_click_pipeline.py for native runs).

  1. The participant profile: match endpoints by type NAME, as Fast DDS 2.x did.

     Fast DDS 3.x (lyrical) can gate an endpoint match on a TypeLookup exchange:
     an announcement carries the remote type's identifier, and if the local
     participant has not registered that type yet it asks the remote for it.
     rmw_fastrtps registers a type when the LOCAL endpoint is created, and
     nav2's servers announce themselves before bt_navigator has built the
     clients that need them, so during a 33-node bringup those lookups are
     routine. TypeLookupManager has no retry and no timeout: one lost reply and
     that endpoint never matches. rmw then reports the server unavailable —
     __rmw_service_server_is_available demands the client's request-writer
     and response-reader match counts be equal and non-zero — for as long as
     anyone waits, while a bystander lists both sides in the graph. That was
     bt_navigator's "compute_path_to_pose / spin / is_path_valid action server
     not available after waiting for 30.00s", on 8 of 13 lyrical runs on
     2026-09-19, the endpoint varying run to run. registration_only strips the
     type information from the announcement (EDP.cpp, TYPEPROPAGATION_
     REGISTRATION_ONLY): types are still registered locally, matching falls
     back to the name, and the lookup path is never entered. 8 of 8 with it.
     Fast DDS 2.14 (jazzy) has no such gate and ignores the property.

  2. The "service" data_writer / data_reader profiles: a 10 s ceiling on the
     reply-writer match.

     rmw_fastrtps will not send a service response until the server's response
     writer has matched the client's response reader, and it waits at most
     max_blocking_time for that match (the "workaround for the race on the
     discovery of services" in rmw_fastrtps_shared_cpp/src/rmw_response.cpp).
     Fast DDS defaults that to 100 ms. Bringing nav2 up starts a crowd of
     participants at once and the match routinely takes longer; the reply is
     then dropped with "failed to send response to /NODE/change_state
     (timeout)", and lifecycle_manager waits forever. rmw looks up a datawriter
     profile by topic name and, failing that, one named exactly "service"
     (rmw_fastrtps_cpp/src/rmw_service.cpp), so this applies to service
     endpoints only and leaves ordinary topics alone. It is a ceiling, not a
     delay: the write proceeds the moment the match happens. Diagnosed on
     linorobot2's console branch (eae75c8, 0c6c779); rmw 9.x still honours it.

  3. The transports: shared memory with a 4 MiB segment, and UDPv4.

     A depth camera publishes one 640x480 16UC1 frame (614 KB) 30 times a
     second; Fast DDS's built-in shared-memory segment is 512 KB, smaller than
     one frame. Fast DDS 3.x (lyrical) then delivered a third of them or less:
     10-12 Hz of a 30 Hz stream between two processes on one machine, 2-9 Hz of
     the simulated Astra Pro's under a full stack, below the 8 Hz the topic check
     asks of /scan, and an Astra exploration that does pass the check finds its
     way home on a costmap fed at a third of the rate. Fast DDS 2.14 (jazzy)
     delivered 28 Hz of the same stream. With the segment at 1, 2 or 4 MiB both
     distros deliver all 30 Hz (lyrical 30.0-30.1, jazzy 26-28: its old rate).
     4 MiB holds a frame and a 640x480 colour image beside it. The pages of a
     segment are only taken as they are written (/dev/shm is the host's, ipc:
     host), so a bigger segment costs what the traffic uses.

     Naming any transport replaces the built-in set, so UDPv4 is listed again:
     discovery, and every peer on another machine, go over it exactly as before.
     A peer on the built-in transports (a desktop's rviz, the other distro)
     still matches (30 Hz both ways, tested). Not LARGE_DATA: a participant on it
     and one on the built-in set exchange nothing at all.
     The descriptors sit inside <profiles>: Fast DDS 3.x rejects the whole file
     ("Not expected tag: 'transport_descriptors'") anywhere else and runs on its
     defaults without another word.
-->
<dds xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
  <profiles>
    <transport_descriptors>
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>cockpit_shm</transport_id>
        <type>SHM</type>
        <segment_size>4194304</segment_size>
      </transport_descriptor>
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>cockpit_udp4</transport_id>
        <type>UDPv4</type>
      </transport_descriptor>
    </transport_descriptors>
    <participant profile_name="cockpit_participant" is_default_profile="true">
      <rtps>
        <userTransports>
          <transport_id>cockpit_shm</transport_id>
          <transport_id>cockpit_udp4</transport_id>
        </userTransports>
        <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
        <propertiesPolicy>
          <properties>
            <property>
              <name>fastdds.type_propagation</name>
              <value>registration_only</value>
            </property>
          </properties>
        </propertiesPolicy>
      </rtps>
    </participant>
    <data_writer profile_name="service">
      <qos>
        <reliability>
          <kind>RELIABLE</kind>
          <max_blocking_time>
            <sec>10</sec>
            <nanosec>0</nanosec>
          </max_blocking_time>
        </reliability>
      </qos>
    </data_writer>
    <data_reader profile_name="service">
      <qos>
        <reliability>
          <kind>RELIABLE</kind>
          <max_blocking_time>
            <sec>10</sec>
            <nanosec>0</nanosec>
          </max_blocking_time>
        </reliability>
      </qos>
    </data_reader>
  </profiles>
</dds>
